{
  "beruf": "pflege",
  "title": "Care work: older people at home and in residences",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Caring for older people is one of the most common jobs for newcomers in Italy, as an assistente familiare (badante) in a private home or as an OSS in a residence. This deck has the people and places, daily care, health and safety, the routine of a shift, the words of your contract and payslip, and how to talk to families and colleagues. In care work you say Lei to older people and to their families, and tu to most colleagues.",
  "introEasy": "Do you care for older people in Italy? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Assistente familiare (badante)",
    "OSS (operatore socio-sanitario)",
    "Assistente domiciliare",
    "Assistente convivente",
    "Assistente notturno"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Say who you are and what you are going to do before you touch someone: «Buongiorno, signora Rossi. Sono Amina. Adesso la aiuto ad alzarsi.» Then wait for the answer.",
    "Use Lei and the surname or title: signor Bianchi, signora Rossi, dottoressa. Many older people like it. Colleagues will mostly say tu to you, and to each other.",
    "Never guess with medication. If you are not sure, ask: «Mi può confermare la dose, per favore?» Write down what happened and tell the next shift at the passaggio di consegne.",
    "Ask for a written contract and a payslip (busta paga) from the first day, and check that contributions go to INPS. Rules for domestic work and for residences are different, and they change by contract (CCNL) and region.",
    "Working as an OSS needs a recognised qualification, and the rules differ by region. For a job as assistente familiare in a private home, ask what is required. Ask the employer, a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
  "links": [
